            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A place or state of deep darkness and temporary confinement where fallen angels are held in chains awaiting final judgment.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"2 Peter 2:4\",\"Jude 1:6\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A general synonym for Hell or Gehenna, representing the final and eternal state of punishment for all wicked beings, both human and angelic.\",\"basis\":[\"2 Peter 2:4\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A synonym for Hades or Sheol, representing the intermediate state of the human dead before the resurrection.\",\"basis\":[\"Classical Greek mythology\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A metaphorical description of the spiritual degradation, limitation, and separation from God experienced by fallen angels, rather than a literal spatial location.\",\"basis\":[\"Theological extrapolation\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In biblical studies, the verb tartaroō is a hapax legomenon in the New Testament. Scholarly consensus strongly distinguishes Tartarus from Gehenna (the final lake of fire) and Hades (the intermediate state for humans), recognizing it instead as a specific holding place for rebellious angels, a concept heavily influenced by Second Temple Jewish apocalyptic traditions such as 1 Enoch.\"}",
